---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import CodeGroup from "../../../components/CodeGroup.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
import Theming from "./_theming.astro"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships first-class Vue components. Install the package, import what you
    need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="installation">Install via NPM</h2>
    <p>
      The components ship as uncompiled single-file components (<code>.vue</code
      > and <code>.ts</code>), so your build has to compile them. Any setup with
      Vue SFC support works, for example Vite with <code
        >@vitejs/plugin-vue</code
      >, Nuxt, or Astro with <code>@astrojs/vue</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "pnpm",
          code: "pnpm add opui-css open-props vue -S",
          lang: "sh",
        },
        {
          title: "npm",
          code: "npm install opui-css open-props vue -S",
          lang: "sh",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="css">Import the CSS</h2>
    <p>
      Vue components ship markup only - the CSS still has to be imported once,
      in the entry file that creates your app. Import everything:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.ts
import { createApp } from "vue"
import "opui-css/css/imports.css"
import App from "./App.vue"

createApp(App).mount("#app")`}
      lang="ts"
    />
    <p>
      Or pick and choose in a CSS file, and import that file in <code
        >main.ts</code
      > instead:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`/* src/styles/main.css */
@import "opui-css/css/layers.css";
@import "opui-css/open-props.css";
@import "opui-css/core/palette.css";
@import "opui-css/css/theme.css";
@import "opui-css/core/normalize.css";
@import "opui-css/css/components.css";
@import "opui-css/core/utils.css";`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.ts
import "./styles/main.css"`}
      lang="ts"
    />
    <p>
      In Nuxt, add the file to <code>css</code> in <code>nuxt.config.ts</code> instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="usage">How to use</h2>
    <Code
      code={`<script setup lang="ts">
import { Button } from "opui-css/vue"
</script>

<template>
  <Button variant="filled">Click me</Button>
</template>`}
      lang="vue"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="ssr">Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Without hydration (Nuxt, or Astro with <code>@astrojs/vue</code> and no <code
        >client:*</code
      > directive), every component renders complete HTML. The native controls work
      and submit with their form. A few things only update on the client:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox#indeterminate">Checkbox</DocLink>
        <code>indeterminate</code>: it's a DOM property, so the box looks
        unchecked. Or call <code>activateIndeterminate()</code> from <code
          >opui-css/css/js/checkbox.js</code
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/range#value">Range</DocLink> value: the <code
          >&lt;output&gt;</code
        > doesn't follow the thumb. The track fill is CSS, so it's fine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>v-model</code> on <DocLink href="/components/checkbox"
          >Checkbox</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/select#classic-select"
          >Classic select</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/radio">Radio</DocLink>, <DocLink
          href="/components/range">Range</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/select">Select</DocLink>, <DocLink
          href="/components/switch">Switch</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/text-field">Text field</DocLink> and <DocLink
          href="/components/textarea">Textarea</DocLink
        >: the ref stays put. The native value still changes and submits.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hydrate those (<code>client:load</code> in Astro) or use them in a client-rendered
      Vue app. Component pages say what needs hydration next to the browser support
      chips, and the API tables mark each prop with a crossed-out server icon.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Theming />
</Guide>
